/*
 * Static Documentation Center generator. See CONTENT_MODEL.md.
 * Run from the repository root: node docs/dc/generate-docs.js
 */
const fs = require("fs");
const path = require("path");

const groups = [
  ["Usage and Maintenance", "usage-maintenance.html", "Install, update, and maintain phytozome GO.", [
    ["Using the Software on Windows", "using-windows.html", `
      <h2>Install and start</h2><p>Download the Windows release archive from the project's GitHub Releases page, extract the complete archive to a local disk, and start <b>phytozome-go.exe</b>. Do not run the program from inside the archive: the application keeps its cache and bundled components beside the executable.</p><p>Choose a location with ample free space. Reserve at least 50 GB when you expect to install the Symbol Name database, source downloads, local BLAST databases, or tree artifacts. A fast local SSD is preferred for large local searches. You may right-click <b>phytozome-go.exe</b> and create a shortcut, then place that shortcut on the Desktop or Start menu; keep the application folder intact.</p>
      <h2>First launch</h2><p>The packaged helper initializes the application-local <b>.cache</b> directory, checks for a newer packaged release, and can offer the prebuilt Symbol Name database. It then starts the terminal user interface (TUI). Security software may ask for permission for the terminal runtime or a browser preview; allow only the files from the extracted release folder.</p>
      <h2>Network access</h2><p>Updates, connector downloads, release metadata, and optional databases use their official remote services. In regions where GitHub or a source provider is inaccessible, including some networks in mainland China, use a lawful and reliable network route that can reach those services before retrying. The program never needs you to disable certificate validation.</p>`],
    ["Using the Software on Macintosh and Linux", "using-macos-linux.html", `
      <h2>Install and start</h2><p>Download the archive that matches your processor and operating system from GitHub Releases, extract it to a writable local folder, and start the supplied launcher from a terminal. Keep the executable, helper, and bundled files together. Put the folder on a disk with at least 50 GB free if you intend to keep the Symbol Name database or large local data caches.</p><p>Create a shell, dock, desktop, or application-menu shortcut using your desktop environment's normal mechanism. The shortcut should point to the launcher in the extracted folder, not to a copied executable with its companion files omitted.</p>
      <h2>Current platform boundary</h2><p>Keyword search, supported downloads, result review, exports, sessions, and the non-system-tree workflows can run on supported Macintosh and Linux releases. The PHgo system-tree runtime is currently supported only by the packaged Windows amd64 release. On Macintosh and Linux the application reports that system-tree work is unavailable; it does not download a substitute runtime.</p>
      <h2>Using the complete Windows feature set</h2><p>If you need the Windows-only tree workflow, run the Windows release through <a target="_blank" href="https://www.winehq.org/">Wine</a> where it is suitable for your environment. Follow the <a target="_blank" href="https://wiki.winehq.org/Wine_User%27s_Guide">Wine User's Guide</a>, create a dedicated prefix, install the complete Windows archive into that prefix, and keep all release files together. Wine is an external compatibility layer; its configuration, graphics drivers, and permissions are outside phytozome GO support.</p>`],
    ["Updating the Software", "updating-software.html", `<h2>How update checks work</h2><p>At packaged startup, the helper can check the project's GitHub Release metadata for a newer bundle appropriate to the current platform. Development builds and explicitly suppressed checks do not behave as release-update checks. An available update is presented for consent; it is not silently installed.</p><h2>What happens when you accept</h2><p>The updater stages the new archive, waits for the running bundle to release its files, preserves user output, verifies key launcher files, replaces the package, and relaunches. A failed verification retains a diagnostic for the next launch instead of claiming success. Do not terminate the updater while it is replacing files.</p><h2>Choose safely</h2><p>Accept a release only from the project's official GitHub Releases page. If you decline, the installed version remains usable. Back up sessions and exported results before a major update; runtime caches can be recreated and should not be copied as a substitute for exports.</p>`],
    ["Updating the Symbol Name Database", "updating-symbol-name-db.html", `<h2>What the database is</h2><p><b>symbolname.pgd</b> is the local, prebuilt Symbol Name database used to rank biologically meaningful aliases from identifiers and structured row metadata. It is not a connector search service and it does not send your labels to an online name-matching API. A missing database can be skipped at startup, but a workflow that requires automatic symbol names asks to install it and cannot silently substitute heuristic names.</p><h2>Install and update</h2><p>The application reads the manifest published by the dedicated <a target="_blank" href="https://github.com/KiriKirby/phytozome-go-symbolname-db">phytozome-go-symbolname-db</a> repository, downloads the referenced release-asset parts, reassembles and decompresses them, and validates the installed database. Progress reports downloaded bytes, total size when available, speed, and installation state. The final file lives in the application-local cache.</p><h2>Safety and recovery</h2><p>Database artifacts are built by the dedicated repository from NCBI Gene source data; end-user installs do not rebuild the database from NCBI. If a manifest, download, checksum, record-count, or source-date validation fails, retry after restoring network access. Do not copy partial part files into the final database path. Keeping 50 GB free avoids installation failures on large databases.</p>`],
    ["Installing and Updating BLAST+ Components", "blast-plus-components.html", `<h2>About BLAST+</h2><p>NCBI BLAST+ is the command-line suite used when a workflow needs a local BLAST database or a dependable fallback to an online server. phytozome GO detects when a requested path needs local BLAST+ and presents an in-product installation prompt rather than assuming the tools are present.</p><h2>Installation behavior</h2><p>Install the component when prompted, then allow the program to validate the required executables before retrying the operation. Local FASTA files and generated BLAST databases are cached and reused; an online-first workflow does not pre-download local assets until fallback is needed. A new application bundle may require the compatible component to be installed again.</p><h2>Source and licence</h2><p>BLAST+ is maintained by the <a target="_blank" href="https://blast.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/doc/blast-help/downloadblastdata.html">National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI)</a>. Its source and licensing information are published by NCBI at <a target="_blank" href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK52628/">BLAST+ documentation</a>. Review those terms before redistributing the component.</p>`],
    ["Troubleshooting Software Issues", "troubleshooting.html", `<h2>Start with the complete diagnostic</h2><p>phytozome GO does not publish a fixed numeric error-code catalogue. Keep the exact message, the selected source and release, the action being performed, and whether the problem occurs again after restart. The following diagnostic classes cover the supported recovery paths.</p><table border="1" width="100%"><tr><th>Diagnostic class</th><th>Meaning and recovery</th></tr><tr><td>Update or package verification</td><td>Download or replacement was incomplete. Restart, retry with free disk space, and obtain the release only from GitHub Releases.</td></tr><tr><td>Symbol Name database validation</td><td>A manifest, part, checksum, decompression, or record validation failed. Retry the database installation; do not use partial files.</td></tr><tr><td>Connector or source download</td><td>The provider is unreachable, changed, rate-limited, or returned invalid data. Check network access, retry later, and retain the exact source URL/message.</td></tr><tr><td>BLAST+ unavailable or command failed</td><td>Install/repair the prompted component, confirm free cache space, then rerun. Local BLAST must use the selected release's matching FASTA.</td></tr><tr><td>PHgo runtime unavailable</td><td>System-tree computation requires the packaged Windows amd64 runtime and its owned MUSCLE binary. Re-extract the complete Windows bundle; on Macintosh/Linux use the stated platform boundary.</td></tr><tr><td>Unknown message</td><td>Record the full text and workflow context. Do not guess at or fabricate an error code; report it with the version and reproducible steps.</td></tr></table><h2>Safe reset</h2><p>Export sessions and results first. The application cache may be regenerated, but deleting it removes downloaded databases and prepared local assets. Do not delete exported output merely to clear a runtime cache.</p>`]
  ]],
  ["Getting Started", "getting-started.html", "Learn the TUI structure and create independent work tabs.", [
    ["Getting Started with the TUI", "tui-introduction.html", `<h2>The terminal user interface</h2><p>The TUI presents pages, forms, tables, task progress, and modal choices in a terminal window. It is keyboard-first and also supports mouse selection where the terminal supports it. Read the instruction line on each page: it identifies the currently available actions and shortcuts.</p><h2>Common interaction</h2><ul><li>Use <b>Tab</b> and <b>Shift+Tab</b> to move between controls.</li><li>Use arrow keys to move within lists, menus, and tables.</li><li>Use <b>Enter</b> to activate the focused command; use <b>Esc</b> or a displayed Back command to leave a page.</li><li>Use visible checkboxes to select rows; table headers and shortcuts may select or clear a group of rows.</li><li>Wait for a labeled task modal to finish, cancel it through its own action when offered, and do not close the terminal during cache, export, or update writes.</li></ul><h2>Accessibility</h2><p>Use a terminal font and window size that makes table columns readable. Resize the terminal before opening a wide result table. The application preserves data in row details and exports even when a table cell is abbreviated for display.</p>`],
    ["Overview of the Home Page", "home-page-overview.html", `<h2>Choose a workflow</h2><p>The home page is the entry point for Keyword Search, BLAST, Explore, and related source selection. It may also offer saved-session entry points and startup information. A workflow begins by choosing a data source and, where required, a species or release.</p><h2>Inputs and release boundaries</h2><p>Keyword pages accept one search item per line. BLAST pages accept sequences, FASTA records, supported source/report URLs, or file-backed input where an <b>Open...</b> action is provided. A selected versioned release is authoritative: its results, FASTA resolution, and local BLAST assets remain within that release rather than falling back to a different database.</p><h2>Task feedback</h2><p>Long actions use progress states such as download preparation, result collection, alias ranking, database construction, or parsing. A progress page is work in progress, not a frozen screen. Retry, Back, and Cancel decisions retain only the state that the relevant page indicates.</p>`],
    ["Building Multiple Tabs", "multiple-tabs.html", `<h2>Independent work areas</h2><p>A tab is an independent workflow context in the terminal session. Use a new tab when you need to compare sources, preserve a result table while beginning another search, or keep different sequence sets separate. Do not assume one tab's temporary selections are visible in another tab.</p><h2>Naming and organization</h2><p>Name a tab for its purpose, source, or input set—for example, a selected species/release and query group. Keep names concise enough to distinguish in the terminal tab bar. Save a session when the work must survive a restart; a tab name alone is not an export or a durable snapshot.</p><h2>Resource considerations</h2><p>Tabs share the installed application and local cache, so cached release data may be reused. Remote operations remain bounded and source-aware; opening many tabs does not grant unlimited simultaneous requests to an external provider.</p>`]
  ]],
  ["Keyword Search", "keyword-search.html", "Find annotated records from a selected source and review grouped results.", [
    ["Using Keyword Search", "using-keyword-search.html", `<h2>Search workflow</h2><ol><li>Choose the source, species, and release when requested.</li><li>Select the search type appropriate for the identifier, locus, symbol, or annotation you have.</li><li>Enter one search term per line and submit the batch.</li><li>Review grouped results in input order, inspect row details, select rows, then export or send selected sequences onward.</li></ol><h2>Precise, wide, and broad search</h2><p>Precise search uses the selected source's exact program for the selected query class. A no-result outcome is meaningful and is not automatically widened. <b>Wide</b> and <b>Broad</b> search are explicit choices for discovery when you want a wider source-defined match. Broad results can be biologically heterogeneous; inspect provenance and sequence availability before transferring rows to BLAST or Canvas.</p><h2>Gene-locus priority</h2><p>For supported NCBI search types, the Gene locus priority selector can query the selected NCBI data or the PLAZA data first for rows that contain a locus. A miss falls back to the row's ordinary NCBI search term. PLAZA-derived rows identify their source and preserve PLAZA provenance; they are not silently represented as NCBI records.</p>`],
    ["Keyword Search Results Table", "keyword-results-table.html", `<h2>Read the table</h2><p>Results are grouped by the input term and normally remain in input order. Use row checkboxes to choose records. The table can show source-specific columns; open row details to see the full schema, identifiers, attributes, provenance, and available FASTA without relying on a shortened cell display.</p><h2>Columns and aliases</h2><p><b>phgo_alias</b> is the ranked alias list produced by the local Symbol Name database. Source <b>alias</b>, <b>symbols</b>, and <b>synonyms</b> remain source data and are retained in details and exports. Alias-like table cells display the first five values followed by an ellipsis when more are present; this is a display limit only.</p><h2>Actions</h2><p>Use filtering, sorting, group navigation, details, and selection commands shown on the page. Selected rows can be exported, moved to Canvas, or prepared as BLAST query sources when a sequence is available. Sequence preparation de-duplicates equivalent fetches and reports its progress; an unavailable sequence must be resolved or excluded before a sequence workflow can continue.</p>`],
    ["Keyword Export", "keyword-export.html", `<h2>Export selected evidence</h2><p>Keyword exports preserve the selected rows and their full available metadata, including source-specific columns and provenance. Supported output includes spreadsheet data, FASTA where sequences are available, PDF analysis reports, and session snapshots. Choose the destination folder in the operating-system picker; the default is an <b>output</b> folder next to the application when a picker is unavailable.</p><h2>FASTA headers</h2><p>Generated FASTA headers are normalized at the final write boundary so they contain no spaces, tabs, or line breaks. This does not change how externally supplied legacy FASTA headers are read. Use the header mode offered by the export page and retain the spreadsheet or session alongside FASTA when you need complete row provenance.</p><h2>Scope</h2><p>Exports use the current selection unless the page explicitly offers an all-rows option. An optional folder name is a subfolder of the folder you choose, not a forced path under the application directory.</p>`],
    ["Keyword Search Connector Documentation", "keyword-connectors.html", `<h2>Connector contract</h2><p>Connectors isolate each provider's acquisition and parsing logic from the shared workflow. The result model records source database, release/species context, biological identifiers, structured metadata, and authentic row-level source URLs only when the source supplied them. The program does not manufacture a gene report URL from an identifier.</p><table border="1" width="100%"><tr><th>Connector</th><th>Documented source behavior</th></tr><tr><td>Phytozome</td><td>Provider-specific retrieval is isolated under the Phytozome source connector.</td></tr><tr><td>CYP/P450</td><td>Downloads and verifies the independently published <code>p450phgo.pgd</code>, then performs species selection and keyword search entirely locally. Category prefixes <code>a:</code>, <code>p:</code>, <code>f:</code>, and <code>b:</code> restrict a query.</td></tr><tr><td>Lemna</td><td>Release download directories are authoritative for available releases and downloadable GFF3, AHRD, and FASTA assets.</td></tr><tr><td>TAIR</td><td>Versioned workflows use only the selected release's configured official assets; a local miss is not replaced with another release.</td></tr><tr><td>NCBI</td><td>Uses the selected NCBI search type; eligible rows can use explicit Gene locus priority.</td></tr><tr><td>PLAZA</td><td>Uses public versioned FTP data for locus resolution and preserves <code>plaza_*</code> provenance fields.</td></tr></table><p>Connector availability depends on each provider and network. Cache reuse and request coalescing reduce duplicate work but do not change a provider's access terms or rate limits.</p>`]
  ]],
  ["BLAST", "blast.html", "Compare sequences with online capabilities or selected-release local fallback databases.", [
    ["Using BLAST", "using-blast.html", `<h2>BLAST workflow</h2><ol><li>Supply one or more sequence sources: FASTA, a sequence, supported report/source URLs, or rows transferred from Keyword Search.</li><li>Choose the target source, species/release, compatible program, and options.</li><li>Run the shown online path when available or accept the local BLAST+ fallback when required.</li><li>Review each query run, select hit rows, and export or send them to Canvas.</li></ol><h2>Query labels and hit labels</h2><p>A label selected for a query source is query metadata. Each BLAST hit has its own label and label-acquisition type; it is not automatically given the query label. Keyword-to-BLAST transfer retains reusable query metadata and sequences, but does not treat raw source aliases and synonyms as new hit-label candidates.</p><h2>Reliable execution</h2><p>Local execution downloads only the FASTA needed by the selected release, builds/reuses a matching local database, runs BLAST+, parses results, and reports each stage. Cancellation waits for the associated task to finish before returning the TUI to normal control.</p>`],
    ["All BLAST Modes", "blast-modes.html", `<h2>Program matrix</h2><table border="1" width="100%"><tr><th>Query</th><th>Database</th><th>Program</th></tr><tr><td>Nucleotide</td><td>Nucleotide</td><td>blastn</td></tr><tr><td>Nucleotide</td><td>Protein</td><td>blastx</td></tr><tr><td>Protein</td><td>Nucleotide</td><td>tblastn</td></tr><tr><td>Protein</td><td>Protein</td><td>blastp</td></tr></table><h2>Online and local paths</h2><p>The capability layer determines whether the chosen provider exposes a compatible online database and whether an official local FASTA exists. When both are available, the workflow prefers the online server first. If server submission or result retrieval is not dependable, it offers the selected-release local BLAST+ fallback. It never fabricates a database from a different release.</p><h2>Availability examples</h2><p>Lemna capability detection uses release download assets and concrete server selectors. TAIR versioned BLAST uses only selected-release official protein/nucleotide FASTA; TAIR12 BLAST remains unavailable until its matching official bulk FASTA is wired. The available-program list preserves provider order and starts on the first available choice.</p>`],
    ["Family Merger", "family-merger.html", `<h2>Purpose</h2><p>Family Merger groups compatible BLAST query sources into family-oriented review where the workflow has already produced ranked alias information. It is a query/source grouping feature, not a rule for overwriting each hit row's independent label.</p><h2>Grouping rules</h2><p>Groups are defined by the genes or sequences used as BLAST queries, including their stored query label and ranked aliases. The merger reuses those stored alias lists; it does not contact the Symbol Name database again. Review family membership before export, because sequence similarity does not by itself establish a biological family definition.</p><h2>Multi-query behavior</h2><p>A workflow that began with multiple query items stays in multi-file/multi-run review mode even if merging leaves a single visible table. Export scope therefore follows the original query count, not the current number of tables.</p>`],
    ["Cross-Database Enhancer", "cross-database-enhancer.html", `<h2>Purpose</h2><p>The Cross-Database Enhancer enriches workflow context using structured, source-backed fields already obtained for the selected result path. It is designed to make source, sequence, and provenance differences visible when comparing databases.</p><h2>Boundaries</h2><p>Enhancement does not invent report links, convert identifiers between biological classes without source evidence, or use a second database as a hidden fallback for a selected release. Fields such as geneid, protein_id, and transcript are displayed according to what they biologically represent.</p><h2>Review before reuse</h2><p>Use row details and exported provenance to verify an enhanced result. A source-specific field may be absent because the selected release does not publish it; absence is preferable to a cross-release guess.</p>`],
    ["BLAST Results Table", "blast-results-table.html", `<h2>Runs and tables</h2><p>Each submitted query can retain its own result table. Multi-query workflows remain multi-run review even when filters, errors, skips, or family merging reduce visible hits. Use the run selector and the table's visible filtering, sorting, detail, and selection commands to inspect results without losing the source query context.</p><h2>Hit fields</h2><p>Read query metadata separately from hit metadata. <b>phgo_alias</b> on a BLAST result is the hit's ranked alias list; source/query aliases remain query metadata. The application uses biologically explicit names such as <b>geneid</b>, <b>protein_id</b>, <b>transcript</b>, and <b>blast_transcript</b> rather than exposing generic internal tracking identifiers.</p><h2>Selection and transfer</h2><p>Choose hit rows with the table controls, open details for complete field values, then export or add rows to Canvas. Off-screen rows are not eagerly redrawn solely for selection changes, which keeps large tables responsive.</p>`],
    ["BLAST Export", "blast-export.html", `<h2>Export scope</h2><p>BLAST export can ask whether to export the current table or all tables when the workflow originally contained more than one query. This protects multi-run evidence even when only one table is currently visible. Exports include the query metadata required to interpret the hits.</p><h2>Formats</h2><p>Use the output page for spreadsheet, FASTA, PDF report, and session snapshot options supported by the current workflow. The system folder picker selects the destination; a requested subfolder is placed inside that destination. FASTA export uses whitespace-safe headers and keeps the detailed identifiers/provenance in companion structured output.</p><h2>Reproducibility</h2><p>Save a session for a durable record of work in progress and keep the selected release, program, query input, options, and exported table together when reporting a result.</p>`],
    ["BLAST Connector Documentation", "blast-connectors.html", `<h2>Capability-first design</h2><p>BLAST connectors publish capabilities to the shared workflow: online nucleotide/protein database availability, IDs required for server submission, and official local nucleotide/protein FASTA availability. The workflow presents valid query/database combinations rather than forcing unsupported choices.</p><table border="1" width="100%"><tr><th>Connector</th><th>Execution contract</th></tr><tr><td>Lemna</td><td>Uses download releases as the authoritative asset list. It tries a reliable server path first and uses matching local FASTA plus BLAST+ as fallback.</td></tr><tr><td>TAIR</td><td>Uses only the selected release's configured official FASTA for versioned local BLAST. No cross-release or live-service sequence fallback is used.</td></tr><tr><td>Other source connectors</td><td>Keep provider parsing isolated and return normalized capabilities/results to the common table workflow.</td></tr></table><p>Server-side forms can change. A capability discovery or submission failure is reported as such; it is not represented as a successful empty result.</p>`]
  ]],
  ["Workflow Reference", "workflow-reference.html", "Understand every main interface, input contract, review control, enrichment stage, and output boundary.", [
    ["Field, State, and File Glossary", "glossary.html", `<h2>Identity and provenance fields</h2><table border="1" width="100%"><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td><code>source_database</code></td><td>The connector that supplied the row, such as Phytozome, lemna, TAIR, NCBI, PLAZA, CYP, or imported FASTA.</td></tr><tr><td>species / genome / release</td><td>The biological organism and versioned dataset context. Similar labels do not make two releases interchangeable.</td></tr><tr><td><code>geneid</code> / Gene locus</td><td>A gene-level identifier or locus name. It is not automatically a protein accession.</td></tr><tr><td><code>transcript</code></td><td>A transcript/isoform identifier; one gene may have several.</td></tr><tr><td><code>protein_id</code> / sequence ID</td><td>The translated protein or provider sequence record used for protein FASTA/BLAST.</td></tr><tr><td><code>gene_report_url</code></td><td>An authentic row page explicitly supplied by input/provider—not a URL constructed from an identifier.</td></tr></table><h2>Naming fields</h2><table border="1" width="100%"><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td><code>label_name</code></td><td>The selected short biological/display label for a result row.</td></tr><tr><td><code>labelname_type</code></td><td>How that label was obtained: user choice, source field, or automatic Symbol Name ranking.</td></tr><tr><td><code>phgo_alias</code></td><td>Ranked alias candidates returned by the local Symbol Name database.</td></tr><tr><td>alias / symbols / synonyms</td><td>Raw provider metadata retained as evidence; not the same as PHgo's ranked list.</td></tr><tr><td><code>display_name</code></td><td>Editable Canvas/tree/MSA visible name. It can duplicate another row and is separate from the source label.</td></tr><tr><td><code>family_name</code></td><td>Derived Family BLAST review/export group name; it never overwrites hit <code>label_name</code>.</td></tr></table><h2>Coordinates and row states</h2><p>A PHgo coordinate <code>[item,row]</code> is one-based visual Canvas identity. Tree runtime taxon IDs such as <code>PHGOT000001</code> are stable internal mapping keys, not biological accessions. Green is selected, yellow is excluded through MSA Apply, and red is ordinarily unchecked. Filter flags mark suggested removal independently of whether the user later changes the checkbox.</p><h2>BLAST evidence terms</h2><p><b>identity</b> is exact matches over the aligned region. <b>query coverage</b> is aligned query length divided by full query length. <b>E-value</b> estimates expected chance hits; lower is stronger. <b>bitscore</b> is a normalized alignment score; higher is stronger. <b>target/query length ratio</b> and <b>target/UniProt canonical length ratio</b> use different denominators and must not be interpreted as the same quality test.</p><h2>File and artifact definitions</h2><table border="1" width="100%"><tr><th>Extension/artifact</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td><code>.fasta</code></td><td>Sequence records. Header detail depends on selected mode.</td></tr><tr><td><code>.xlsx</code></td><td>Full structured exported result rows.</td></tr><tr><td><code>.pgo</code></td><td>Resumable PHgo workflow/session snapshot.</td></tr><tr><td><code>.pgv</code></td><td>Reactree viewer snapshot; it does not contain the complete Canvas workflow.</td></tr><tr><td><code>.nwk</code></td><td>Newick tree structure.</td></tr><tr><td><code>input.fasta</code></td><td>Stable-ID FASTA handed to the MEGA runtime.</td></tr><tr><td><code>aligned.fasta</code></td><td>Runtime-produced alignment shared with tree/MSA viewers.</td></tr><tr><td><code>runtime-request.json</code></td><td>Selected methods and all editable runtime parameters.</td></tr><tr><td><code>runtime-response.json</code></td><td>Runtime result, error text, artifacts, and reported skipped records.</td></tr><tr><td><code>viewer.payload.json</code></td><td>Newick, alignment, record metadata and display mapping sent to viewers.</td></tr><tr><td><code>run.manifest.json</code></td><td>Fingerprints and artifact paths governing safe reuse.</td></tr></table><h2>Storage definitions</h2><p><code>.cache/</code> is replaceable working data. <code>output/</code> or a chosen export folder contains deliverables. A cache hit changes speed, not scientific meaning. <code>~</code> means an intentional empty PHgo FASTA/header field; it is not the literal biological value tilde.</p>`],
    ["Main Interface and Data Sources", "main-interface-data-sources.html", `<h2>The three main tabs</h2><table border="1" width="100%"><tr><th>Tab</th><th>What it starts</th><th>Required input</th></tr><tr><td>Keyword</td><td>Annotation/identifier searches in Phytozome, CYP/P450, lemna, TAIR, or NCBI.</td><td>Database, applicable search type/species, and one or more search terms.</td></tr><tr><td>BLAST</td><td>Sequence similarity searches in Phytozome, lemna, or TAIR.</td><td>Target database/species, compatible program where offered, and sequence/FASTA/URL rows.</td></tr><tr><td>Explore</td><td>Open a <code>.pgo</code> session, create a blank Canvas, or open a <code>.nwk/.pgv</code> viewer file.</td><td>A file for Open session/viewer, or no input for New canvas.</td></tr></table><h2>Keyword source capability matrix</h2><table border="1" width="100%"><tr><th>Source</th><th>Species</th><th>Search behavior</th><th>Important boundary</th></tr><tr><td>Phytozome</td><td>Required</td><td>Precise and explicit Wide search.</td><td>Provider parsing stays in the Phytozome connector.</td></tr><tr><td>CYP/P450</td><td>Required or assigned per row</td><td>Local search of the installed <code>p450phgo.pgd</code>.</td><td>It does not scrape Dr Nelson pages at runtime. Prefixes <code>a:</code>, <code>p:</code>, <code>f:</code>, <code>b:</code> restrict the category.</td></tr><tr><td>lemna</td><td>Required</td><td>Release GFF3 plus AHRD annotation search.</td><td>The download release directory is authoritative.</td></tr><tr><td>TAIR</td><td>Version required</td><td>Selected-release local assets; TAIR12 uses its configured ENA path.</td><td>A miss never silently searches another TAIR version.</td></tr><tr><td>NCBI</td><td>Not required</td><td>Chosen Entrez search type through E-utilities.</td><td>Search-type fields differ. Eligible types expose Gene locus priority through NCBI or PLAZA.</td></tr></table><h2>BLAST source capability matrix</h2><p>Phytozome, lemna, and TAIR are the implemented main-interface BLAST targets. The program list is capability driven. Lemna may try its server first and fall back to matching release FASTA plus local BLAST+. Historical TAIR releases use their configured local assets only. Availability is a property of the chosen release; PHgo does not borrow a database from another release.</p><h2>Species controls</h2><p>A species key is internal identity while the visible label is the scientific/release name. Search filters candidates but does not change the selected release. CYP Custom assigns species in the row grid; Set all/Set remaining applies a chosen fallback. Rows in Custom mode may inherit the most recent earlier non-empty species, which is why the first active row must have an explicit species.</p>`],
    ["Input Formats and Batch Rules", "input-formats.html", `<h2>Keyword grid</h2><p>Each executable row needs a Search term. Symbol name is user-facing metadata and may be automatically identified; Gene locus appears only for search types that support it. Empty rows are ignored. The main grid removes control whitespace from ordinary fields, and <code>~</code> is an explicit blank placeholder in legacy/batch label flows.</p><h2>BLAST input forms</h2><ul><li>Raw nucleotide or amino-acid sequence.</li><li>One or more FASTA records.</li><li>A supported Phytozome report URL or lemna JBrowse URL.</li><li>Rows transferred from Keyword Search.</li><li>A local file selected with <b>Open...</b>, or supported legacy <code>load "file.txt"</code> input.</li></ul><p>For multiple queries, use one query per line unless supplying multi-line FASTA records. If labels are pasted as a parallel batch, provide one for every item or omit all of them; use <code>~</code> for an intentionally blank label. Generated FASTA headers contain no whitespace, but imported external headers may contain spaces.</p><h2>Program choice</h2><table border="1" width="100%"><tr><th>Program</th><th>Query interpreted as</th><th>Database interpreted as</th></tr><tr><td>BLASTN</td><td>Nucleotide</td><td>Nucleotide/genome</td></tr><tr><td>BLASTX</td><td>Nucleotide translated in reading frames</td><td>Protein</td></tr><tr><td>TBLASTN</td><td>Protein</td><td>Translated nucleotide/genome</td></tr><tr><td>BLASTP</td><td>Protein</td><td>Protein</td></tr></table><p>Choosing a program does not biologically repair an incompatible query. Preserve the original source URL and identifiers when reproducibility matters.</p><h2>Label names and aliases</h2><p><b>label_name</b> is the selected short name; <b>labelname_type</b> records how it was obtained; <b>phgo_alias</b> is a ranked alias list from the local Symbol Name database. Raw provider aliases, symbols, and synonyms remain source evidence. A manually entered or explicitly selected label is authoritative and is not silently replaced later.</p>`],
    ["Result Tables and Keyboard Controls", "result-table-controls.html", `<h2>Cell-based review</h2><p>Selection focus is one cell, not an entire row. Checkbox and row-number columns remain fixed while data columns scroll horizontally. Green means checked and red means unchecked. The checkbox in the first header cell toggles all normal rows. Keyword group-title rows are not selectable and preserve input-term order.</p><h2>Keyboard model</h2><table border="1" width="100%"><tr><th>Control</th><th>Effect</th></tr><tr><td>Arrows</td><td>Move through body cells; horizontal navigation brings complete columns into view.</td></tr><tr><td>Space</td><td>Toggle the focused row checkbox where applicable.</td></tr><tr><td>Tab</td><td>Switch between body-cell control and sortable column-header control.</td></tr><tr><td>Left/Right in header control</td><td>Move only among sortable headers.</td></tr><tr><td>Up/Down in header control</td><td>Change only the current column's sort direction.</td></tr><tr><td>Enter / View</td><td>Open full details for the focused data row; in header control, explain the focused column.</td></tr><tr><td>Ctrl+Shift+C / Copy</td><td>Copy only the focused normal cell. Success is silent.</td></tr><tr><td>Ctrl+L / Aliases</td><td>When a <code>label_name</code> cell is focused, choose or rename its alias.</td></tr><tr><td>Ctrl+G / Export</td><td>Open export settings; multi-run pages first ask Current table or All tables.</td></tr></table><h2>Sorting and display</h2><p>Default order is original row number ascending. Keyword sorting happens inside each search-term group and never moves a row to another group. Display-column choices affect only the table; details and exports retain the full underlying schema. BLAST ratio headers may occupy two physical header rows so numerator and denominator remain readable.</p><h2>Invalid actions</h2><p>View or Copy on a group title, checkbox, or otherwise non-data cell produces a small explanatory modal instead of changing workflow state. Export and transfer always use checkbox state, not merely the currently focused row.</p>`],
    ["External References, Family BLAST, and Filtering", "blast-analysis-controls.html", `<h2>External-reference stage</h2><p>BLAST can independently enable automatic hit Symbol Name identification, UniProt enrichment, and InterPro enrichment. UniProt adds accession/canonical-length and review evidence. InterPro resolves conserved-region evidence through the official EBI API. Missing evidence remains blank; PHgo does not invent a weak mapping. Results are cached and duplicate lookups are coalesced.</p><h2>InterPro conserved-region defaults</h2><p>The status evaluator uses Pfam, InterPro and signature accessions, entry type, coverage, and match regions by default; entry-name matching is off. <b>present</b> requires at least 70% coverage and one matched item, while <b>partial</b> starts at 25% coverage and one matched item. These thresholds are editable in the external-reference window.</p><h2>Family BLAST</h2><p>When at least two query labels share a detectable family pattern, PHgo offers Family BLAST. Default behavior groups by detected prefix, strips leading species text, trailing member indexes, number-following suffixes and terminal subtype suffixes, normalizes inner punctuation, then merges duplicate target rows and keeps the preferred hit. Minimum group size is 2. Grouping and merging are separate: turning merging off keeps the family unit but retains all member hit rows. The derived <b>family_name</b> does not rewrite hit <b>label_name</b>.</p><h2>When Filter is available</h2><p>The automatic BLAST Filter appears only when both UniProt and InterPro reference columns are present for the run. Applying it changes suggested checkbox state and marks suggested removals; it does not delete rows. Manual checkbox changes remain possible, and rerunning the filter restores its current recommendation.</p><h2>Default hard decisions</h2><ul><li>Identity, query coverage, and E-value limits default to 0/off.</li><li>Canonical target length is required and accepted at 70–130% when that evidence exists for the program.</li><li>InterPro conserved region is required; present and partial are accepted, missing and uncertain are rejected.</li><li>UniProt accession and reviewed status are not hard requirements by default.</li><li>The best isoform per target gene is retained; top-N limiting is off.</li><li>Soft scoring is off. Ranking still uses score, identity, coverage, reference evidence, E-value, then bitscore as configured tie-breakers.</li></ul><h2>Strong evidence fallback</h2><p>For rows missing reference anchors, the default fallback may retain a very strong BLAST result: identity at least 40%, E-value at most <code>1e-80</code>, and target/query length 80–120%. Family-consensus and semantic-agreement requirements are separately configurable. Review these rules before applying them to nucleotide/genome-target programs, where protein canonical-length evidence may not be biologically equivalent.</p>`],
    ["Exports, Reports, Cache, and Recovery", "exports-cache-recovery.html", `<h2>Export settings</h2><p>Export begins only after the Export action. The modal collects file name, optional subfolder, enabled formats, FASTA header mode, all-rows choice where supported, and PDF data-analysis report choice. The operating-system folder picker chooses the destination; if unavailable, PHgo uses <code>output/</code> beside the executable. An entered folder name is a subfolder of that chosen destination.</p><h2>Output types</h2><table border="1" width="100%"><tr><th>Output</th><th>Purpose</th></tr><tr><td>XLSX</td><td>Full structured rows, including fields not visible in the table.</td></tr><tr><td>FASTA</td><td>Selected sequences using the chosen PHgo/original/minimal header contract.</td></tr><tr><td>PDF report</td><td>Structured analysis context, settings, provenance and sequence audit—not the removed plain-text log.</td></tr><tr><td>PGO session</td><td>Resumable workflow state; not a replacement for portable result exports.</td></tr><tr><td>NWK/PGV and graphics</td><td>Tree structure/viewer snapshot and viewer-owned graphic outputs.</td></tr></table><h2>Cache versus output</h2><p><code>.cache/</code> beside the application holds replaceable runtime data: provider downloads, prepared BLAST databases, symbol-name data, and tree runs under <code>.cache/tree/</code>. <code>output/</code> and user-selected folders hold deliverables. Clearing cache may force expensive downloads/rebuilds but must not be used as a way to delete exports.</p><h2>Cancellation and recovery</h2><p>Long operations use a task/progress modal with Cancel. Cancellation stops the task context and waits for its workers before returning to the previous page. Expected failures offer applicable Retry, Skip, Back, or Exit actions. In batch work, Skip applies to the failing item where safe; completed items are preserved. Empty search results are recoverable outcomes, not process crashes.</p><h2>Source authenticity</h2><p>A row-level source URL is recorded only when the exact URL came from user input or an explicit provider response. PHgo does not synthesize a plausible gene page from an identifier. Release/download/API URLs remain separate provenance fields.</p>`]
  ]],
  ["Explore", "explore.html", "Preserve work, curate sequences, and examine tree and alignment artifacts.", [
    ["Standalone Tree Viewer", "standalone-tree-viewer.html", `<h2>Accepted input</h2><p>Choose <b>Explore → Tree viewer browser</b> and provide exactly one local <code>.nwk</code> or <code>.pgv</code> path, a <code>file://</code> link, or an HTTP(S) URL ending in one of those formats. <code>.nwk</code> supplies Newick tree structure; <code>.pgv</code> is a PHgo viewer snapshot carrying durable Reactree view/edit state. A <code>.pgv</code> is not a Canvas <code>.pgo</code> workflow session.</p><h2>Independent sessions</h2><p>Each submission opens an independent local browser viewer service and page. It does not share Canvas viewer state or computation artifacts. Leaving the browser-input screen stops its local service; save/export viewer work before leaving.</p><h2>Viewer commands</h2><p>Reactree owns layout, search, labels, branch display, graphical export, and node editing. Reroot, Flip, Swap, and Color are persistent interaction modes: click the same command again, choose another mode, or press Escape to exit. Midpoint and Ladderize are immediate one-shot commands. Quoted Newick labels preserve underscores and punctuation rather than silently rewriting display names.</p><h2>What it does not do</h2><p>The standalone viewer does not align sequences or infer a new tree. To compute from sequences, add them to Canvas and use the Windows PHgo system-tree runtime.</p>`],
    ["Sessions", "sessions.html", `<h2>What a session saves</h2><p>A <b>.pgo</b> session snapshot preserves durable workflow state: result data, review state, selected source context, Canvas items, and relevant tree/MSA artifacts and settings. It is intended to let work continue without recreating a search or recomputing an already saved preview.</p><h2>What it does not save</h2><p>Transient UI state—open panels, focus, scroll positions, temporary menus, and browser viewport state—is not part of a session. Export a spreadsheet, FASTA, or report separately when you need a portable deliverable rather than a resumable application session.</p><h2>Opening safely</h2><p>Opening a legacy snapshot can remap old tree restore paths into the application cache. The first user-initiated tree refresh after opening a snapshot is a full runtime computation, even if saved artifacts exist. Keep snapshots with their exported results and record the application version used.</p>`],
    ["Canvas", "canvas.html", `<h2>What Canvas is</h2><p>Canvas is the sequence curation workspace between search results and tree/MSA analysis. A Canvas item may come from Keyword Search, BLAST, or imported FASTA. Its rows retain their source metadata rather than being flattened into anonymous sequences.</p><h2>Selection states</h2><table border="1" width="100%"><tr><th>State</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Tree/MSA behavior</th></tr><tr><td>Green</td><td>Selected in Canvas.</td><td>Sent to both tree and MSA.</td></tr><tr><td>Yellow</td><td>Unchecked by an MSA <b>Apply</b> operation.</td><td>Excluded from both tools, but distinguished from an ordinary unchecked row.</td></tr><tr><td>Red</td><td>Ordinary unchecked row.</td><td>Excluded from both tools.</td></tr></table><p>Tree and MSA deliberately share one selection set. Changing selection is a computation change: the next tree refresh reruns the runtime instead of merely changing labels.</p><h2>Names and coordinates</h2><p><b>display_name</b> is the editable name used by tree/MSA previews. It is not the source gene symbol and does not need to be unique. PHgo keeps the row coordinate separately as <code>[Canvas item, row]</code>, for example <code>[1,13]</code>. MSA always draws that prefix in the left ID list; Reactree shows it only when its <b>Coord</b> viewer switch is on. Neither the coordinate nor MSA's display-only residue ratio is written into <b>display_name</b>.</p><h2>FASTA export procedure</h2><ol><li>Select the rows to export, or enable the explicit all-rows switch.</li><li>Open Export and choose a destination folder.</li><li>Choose the header mode according to whether the file will be round-tripped through Canvas or consumed by another program.</li><li>Keep the session or spreadsheet beside simplified FASTA if provenance matters.</li></ol><p>With all-rows off, normal FASTA export includes selected rows only and filters rows that do not have an export-ready sequence. This is separate from tree input selection: PHgo does not silently drop a selected tree row merely because the ordinary FASTA exporter cannot resolve it.</p><h2>Snapshots</h2><p>A <code>.pgo</code> snapshot preserves Canvas rows, selections, display names, durable tree/MSA state, payloads, and available computation artifacts. It does not preserve open panels, focus, scroll positions, menus, or browser viewport geometry. The first explicit tree refresh after reopening a snapshot always performs a full computation.</p>`],
    ["PHgo FASTA Header Formats", "phgo-fasta-headers.html", `<h2>Choose the format for the next consumer</h2><table border="1" width="100%"><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Generated header</th><th>Use it when</th></tr><tr><td>PHgo Canvas</td><td><code>&gt;phgo://species/label/id\\source_label/source_id\\raw_row/canvas_title\\display_name</code></td><td>You want the richest PHgo round trip, including Canvas position and the locked display name.</td></tr><tr><td>PHgo</td><td><code>&gt;phgo://species/label/id</code> with optional source/row groups</td><td>You need PHgo identity and workflow provenance outside the Canvas-specific form.</td></tr><tr><td>PHgo Lite</td><td><code>&gt;species|ID(symbol)</code> or <code>&gt;species|ID</code></td><td>You need a short, broadly readable header while retaining species and primary identifier.</td></tr><tr><td>Original</td><td>The source/import header, normalized at output.</td><td>A downstream tool or collaborator expects the provider's original identifier convention.</td></tr><tr><td>Minimal ID-only</td><td><code>&gt;ID</code></td><td>The consumer requires a compact stable identifier and provenance is stored elsewhere.</td></tr><tr><td>Display-name-only</td><td><code>&gt;display_name</code></td><td>Canvas labels are the intended human-facing names; this mode is available on Canvas export.</td></tr></table><h2>PHgo Canvas fields</h2><p>The four backslash-separated groups have fixed roles. In the first group, <b>species</b> is the organism/source species label, <b>label</b> is the row's symbol/label name, and <b>id</b> is the best available transcript/protein/gene identifier. The second group records the BLAST/query source label and source identifier when applicable. The third group stores the original Canvas row number and Canvas item title. The fourth group stores the Canvas <b>display_name</b>. On Canvas import, this last value is restored and locked so later automatic display-name-source changes do not overwrite it.</p><h2>Empty fields and reserved separators</h2><p><code>~</code> is the canonical empty-field marker. The parser restores it as empty; the generator does not guess missing species, labels, or source fields. Within full PHgo fields, slash and backslash are separators and are therefore replaced with underscores. PHgo Lite also replaces <code>|</code>, <code>(</code>, and <code>)</code> inside field values. Empty, <code>~</code>, and <code>~~</code> symbols never produce a parenthetical PHgo Lite suffix.</p><h2>Whitespace and compatibility</h2><p>At the final write boundary every PHgo-generated FASTA header replaces spaces, tabs, and line breaks with underscores. Readers remain backward compatible with external and older headers containing spaces. Sequences are written as ordinary FASTA records; tree-runtime FASTA is a separate internal format using stable <code>PHGOT...</code> taxon IDs.</p><h2>Concrete examples</h2><pre>&gt;phgo://Arabidopsis_thaliana/PAL1/AT2G37040.1\\~/~\\7/keyword_results\\PAL1\nMST...\n\n&gt;Arabidopsis_thaliana|AT2G37040.1(PAL1)\nMST...</pre><p>The first example is suited to Canvas re-import. The second is easier to read in generic sequence software but cannot carry Canvas row/title/display metadata.</p>`],
    ["Phylogenetic Trees", "phylogenetic-trees.html", `<h2>Platform and runtime boundary</h2><p>System-tree computation is supported by the packaged Windows amd64 release. PHgo verifies <code>mega-phgo-runtime.bin</code> and its runtime-owned <code>muscleWin64.bin</code> beside the application. It does not search PATH, use an installed MEGA/MEGA-CC, or download a substitute. Macintosh and Linux report this feature as unsupported.</p><h2>Input rules</h2><p>The checked Canvas rows, in visible order, form the run. Protein mode requests protein alignment; DNA mode requires real nucleotide/CDS already embedded or resolvable from the row. PHgo never reverse-translates protein, guesses sequence kind from letters, pads residues, or repairs input. Immediately before runtime FASTA is written it removes one or more terminal <code>*</code> terminators; internal <code>*</code> characters remain visible to MEGA.</p><h2>Alignment choices</h2><table border="1" width="100%"><tr><th>Target</th><th>Methods</th><th>Important controls</th></tr><tr><td>Protein</td><td>ClustalW; MUSCLE</td><td>ClustalW exposes pairwise/multiple gap penalties, protein matrix, residue/hydrophilic penalties, gap separation, divergent cutoff, predefined gaps and guide tree. MUSCLE exposes gap open/extend, hydrophobicity multiplier, memory, iterations and clustering methods.</td></tr><tr><td>DNA</td><td>ClustalW (DNA); MUSCLE (DNA); ClustalW (Codons); MUSCLE (Codons)</td><td>DNA methods expose their nucleotide penalties/matrix controls. Codon methods additionally require coding input and expose a genetic-code table; choosing a codon method does not make non-coding data valid.</td></tr></table><p>Increasing a gap-opening penalty makes starting a gap less favorable; extension controls the cost of lengthening an existing gap. <b>Keep Predefined Gaps</b> preserves gaps already present in the input. <b>Delay Divergent Cutoff</b> postpones sequences above the chosen divergence threshold. MUSCLE <b>Max Iterations</b> trades running time for additional refinement; <b>Max Memory</b> is a runtime limit, not an image/export setting.</p><h2>Tree methods and controls</h2><p>Available methods are Neighbor-Joining, Minimum Evolution, UPGMA, Maximum Likelihood, and Maximum Parsimony. <b>Test of Phylogeny</b> enables bootstrap; <b>Bootstrap Replicates</b> is used only with bootstrap selected. Model/Method chooses the substitution-distance or likelihood model. Rates among sites, gamma settings, gap/missing-data deletion, and site-coverage cutoff determine which evidence reaches inference. ML additionally exposes search heuristic, initial-tree strategy/file, number of initial trees, branch-swap filter, and threads. MP exposes search method, random-addition initial trees, search level, and maximum retained trees.</p><p><b>Number of Threads</b> defaults to one fewer than the logical CPUs, never below one, and cannot exceed the detected CPU count in the UI. More threads mainly affect runtime-backed parallel phases; it does not increase remote-network concurrency.</p><h2>Refresh semantics</h2><table border="1" width="100%"><tr><th>Change</th><th>Next Refresh</th></tr><tr><td>Only display name or display-name source</td><td>Render-only; alignment and tree are reused.</td></tr><tr><td>Selection, sequence, target, alignment method/parameter, tree method/parameter</td><td>Full runtime compute and render.</td></tr><tr><td>Reactree-only view controls such as Coord</td><td>Immediate viewer state; no PHgo refresh or recomputation.</td></tr></table><p>The first refresh in a live Canvas and the first user refresh after opening a snapshot are full computations. Runtime <code>error_text</code> is shown before secondary missing-artifact errors. If MEGA identifies bad records, Skip unchecks the exact mapped Canvas rows and retries; PHgo does not silently switch alignment methods.</p>`],
    ["Multiple Sequence Alignment", "multiple-sequence-alignment.html", `<h2>Open and synchronize the MSA</h2><p>The browser-hosted JalviewJS MSA uses the aligned FASTA from the last successful Canvas refresh; both aligned FASTA and Newick must exist before it opens. Tree and MSA share the same selected rows. The left list always shows the PHgo coordinate before the raw display name and appends a display-only <code>residues/alignment length percent</code> value; those decorations are not part of the sequence name.</p><h2>Save versus Apply</h2><table border="1" width="100%"><tr><th>Action</th><th>Effect</th></tr><tr><td>Save</td><td>Stores durable Jalview state—sequence edits, row states, groups, features, annotations, markers, settings and colours—for a later Canvas snapshot. It does not edit Canvas.</td></tr><tr><td>Apply</td><td>Sends permitted row/name/sequence changes back to Canvas, updates shared artifacts and selection state, and can therefore affect the next tree computation.</td></tr><tr><td>Export image...</td><td>Writes a visual file only. It does not Apply, edit alignment data, or recompute the tree.</td></tr></table><h2>Image formats and dimensions</h2><table border="1" width="100%"><tr><th>Control</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Format</td><td>SVG</td><td>SVG is resolution-independent and best for later editing; PNG is raster; PDF embeds the same vector scene on a page sized from the logical scene.</td></tr><tr><td>Scale</td><td>2x</td><td>PNG-only raster multiplier: 1x, 2x, 5x, or 10x. It multiplies output pixels, not logical layout size, and is disabled for SVG/PDF.</td></tr><tr><td>Cell width / height</td><td>9 / 13 logical px</td><td>Positive numbers controlling every residue cell. Width mainly changes horizontal density; height changes row spacing.</td></tr><tr><td>Column interval</td><td>20</td><td>Positive integer spacing for alignment-column labels when column numbering is enabled.</td></tr></table><h2>Content switches</h2><ul><li><b>Show PHgo coordinates</b> (off): prefixes exported labels with the visible <code>[item,row]</code>.</li><li><b>Show length ratio</b> (off): adds non-gap/non-blank/non-terminal-marker residues over aligned row length.</li><li><b>Show length percent</b> (off): adds the percentage form of that ratio.</li><li><b>Show alignment column numbers</b> (on): draws MSA column numbering using Column interval.</li><li><b>Show right residue numbers</b> (off): draws each row's cumulative ungapped residue endpoint at the right of each block. Advanced layout turns this on automatically.</li><li><b>Show groups</b> and <b>Show features</b> (both on): include durable Jalview group/feature rendering when present.</li></ul><p><b>Refresh preview</b> rebuilds the preview after setting edits. <b>Generate</b> asks for the save target and writes the selected format. The default file base comes from the numbered payload/page title. The exporter creates a clean Jalview-native vector scene; it is not a screenshot and excludes checkboxes, selections, menus, scrollbars and window chrome.</p><h2>Automatic layout</h2><p>With <b>Use advanced layout script</b> off, every current exportable row and the full alignment width are rendered as one block. Use this for ordinary complete-alignment figures.</p><h2>Advanced layout syntax</h2><p>Each non-empty line is <code>&gt;rows\\start,end[/allocation]</code>. There are no comments, tokens cannot contain internal spaces, each line needs exactly one backslash, and parsing is case-sensitive.</p><pre>&gt;1,4/1,3/1,6\\10,100/~,~,~\n&gt;~\\100,200/50,50</pre><p>The first line exports rows <code>[1,4]</code>, <code>[1,3]</code>, and <code>[1,6]</code> in exactly that order, takes boundary range <code>[10,100)</code> (90 alignment columns), and makes three 30-column blocks. Row coordinates are the one-based coordinates printed by PHgo. <code>&gt;~</code> means all current MSA rows and does not filter by green/yellow/red state.</p><h2>Column boundary coordinates</h2><p>Ranges use MSA <em>boundaries</em>, not one-based residue numbers: boundary 0 is before column 1 and boundary <i>W</i> is after the last column. Thus <code>10,100</code> means the half-open range <code>[10,100)</code> and contains 90 columns. In an endpoint, <code>~</code> means the open edge: <code>~,10</code> starts at 0, <code>10,~</code> ends at alignment width, and <code>~,~</code> selects all columns.</p><h2>Block allocation</h2><p>Counts after the slash split the chosen range from left to right. Fixed counts must be positive and sum exactly to the range length. An allocation <code>~</code> shares the remaining columns equally among all placeholders: <code>10,100/30,~,~</code> becomes 30,30,30. If the remainder is not evenly divisible, generation fails with the line number and failing token. Omitting allocation makes one block covering the whole range.</p><h2>Common errors</h2><ul><li>Use <code>1,4</code>, not <code>[1,4]</code>, inside the script.</li><li>A row coordinate must exist in the current MSA payload and cannot repeat within one line.</li><li>Range endpoints must be between 0 and alignment width, with start strictly less than end.</li><li><code>~</code> for all rows is valid only as the complete row side; do not mix it with explicit rows.</li><li>Multiple lines and their allocated blocks render top to bottom in input order.</li></ul>`]
  ]]
];

const sample = fs.readFileSync(path.join(__dirname, "dc_sample.html"), "utf8");
const esc = value => value.replace(/&/g, "&amp;").replace(/</g, "&lt;").replace(/>/g, "&gt;");

// Every focused article ends with a task-oriented guide. The short topic body
// explains the feature; these guides explain how a user actually completes the
// task, interprets internal terms, and recovers from expected failures.
const userGuides = {
  "Field, State, and File Glossary": `<h2>How to use this glossary</h2><p>When two columns look similar, compare the definition before filtering or exporting. Preserve the exact field name in reports, especially for gene, transcript, protein and length-ratio fields. Internal runtime taxon IDs and Canvas coordinates are mapping devices; cite biological identifiers from the source fields instead.</p>`,
  "Using the Software on Windows": `<h2>First-run checklist</h2><ol><li>Extract the entire release archive; do not copy only the launcher.</li><li>Place the folder on a writable local SSD with sufficient free space.</li><li>Start <b>phytozome-go.exe</b> and let startup tab <code>[0]</code> finish update/cache checks.</li><li>If offered, install the Symbol Name database; declining postpones it until a feature needs it.</li><li>Confirm that the main Keyword, BLAST, and Explore tabs appear.</li></ol><h2>Files that must stay together</h2><p>The launcher, <code>core.bin</code>, <code>phgohelper.bin</code>, bundled WezTerm files, and on Windows the PHgo tree runtime belong to one installation. Missing <code>mega-phgo-runtime.bin</code> or <code>muscleWin64.bin</code> disables system-tree work even if MEGA is installed elsewhere.</p><h2>If startup fails</h2><p>Re-extract a clean official archive, check available disk space, retain the exact helper message, and retry. Do not run from inside the ZIP and do not solve a runtime-file error by copying an unrelated executable into the folder.</p>`,
  "Using the Software on Macintosh and Linux": `<h2>Start step by step</h2><ol><li>Extract the matching release archive to a writable folder.</li><li>Keep launcher and companion files together.</li><li>Run the supplied launcher from a terminal or a shortcut whose working folder is the extracted bundle.</li><li>Use Keyword, supported BLAST, export, Canvas, session, and viewer workflows normally.</li></ol><h2>Tree limitation</h2><p>A message that PHgo system-tree is unsupported is expected on these platforms. It is not a missing PATH dependency. Use a supported Windows amd64 bundle when MEGA-backed tree computation is required; standalone Newick viewing is a separate function.</p>`,
  "Updating the Software": `<h2>Safe update procedure</h2><ol><li>Export important tables and save <code>.pgo</code> sessions.</li><li>At startup, read the offered installed/new version information.</li><li>Accept only when the bundle comes from the official GitHub release.</li><li>Let the detached updater finish replacement and relaunch.</li><li>After relaunch, verify the version and open one saved session.</li></ol><h2>Preserved and replaced data</h2><p>The updater preserves user <code>output/</code> and the application-local Symbol Name database. Managed tools and bundled runtimes come from the new release; they are not carried forward blindly. A verification failure means the update did not complete—keep the diagnostic and restart rather than manually mixing two releases.</p>`,
  "Updating the Symbol Name Database": `<h2>When you need it</h2><p>Automatic Symbol Name identification and ranked <code>phgo_alias</code> values need <code>symbolname.pgd</code>. Searches themselves may still work without it, but PHgo cannot honestly replace the missing database with guessed labels.</p><h2>Install procedure</h2><ol><li>Accept the startup prompt or trigger a feature that requests Symbol Names.</li><li>Review source, remote size, modification date, and local target.</li><li>Keep the app open while parts download, reassemble, decompress, and validate.</li><li>Retry if checksum, size, record-count, or manifest validation fails.</li></ol><h2>Internal definition</h2><p><code>label_name</code> is the chosen display label; <code>phgo_alias</code> is the ranked candidate list; provider aliases remain separate evidence. The PGD is prebuilt by its dedicated repository—end-user PHgo never rebuilds it from NCBI.</p>`,
  "Installing and Updating BLAST+ Components": `<h2>When BLAST+ is used</h2><p>Local fallback and release-local BLAST require <code>makeblastdb</code> plus the selected program (<code>blastn</code>, <code>blastx</code>, <code>tblastn</code>, or <code>blastp</code>). Online-first paths do not prepare a local database until fallback is actually needed.</p><h2>What PHgo does</h2><ol><li>Downloads the selected release's matching FASTA.</li><li>Builds a nucleotide or protein database with <code>makeblastdb</code>.</li><li>Caches the complete database for reuse.</li><li>Runs the requested program and parses tabular results.</li></ol><p>If installation or validation fails, repair the prompted component and retry the same operation. Do not point one release at another release's FASTA.</p>`,
  "Troubleshooting Software Issues": `<h2>Information to collect</h2><ul><li>PHgo version and operating system.</li><li>Selected source, species/release, mode, and program.</li><li>The exact error text—not a paraphrase.</li><li>Whether Retry reproduces it and whether other sources work.</li><li>For runtime failures, the relevant artifact/log names when available.</li></ul><h2>Recovery order</h2><ol><li>Use the modal's Retry for transient provider/network failure.</li><li>Use Skip only when losing the current batch item is acceptable.</li><li>Use Back to correct source, species, input, or settings.</li><li>Cancel long work through its modal so workers unwind cleanly.</li><li>Re-extract the official bundle for missing packaged files.</li></ol><p>There is no public numeric error-code catalogue. A source returning no records is different from a transport/parser error and should not be reported as a crash.</p>`,
  "Getting Started with the TUI": `<h2>Practice workflow</h2><ol><li>Use Left/Right or the shown tab controls to select Keyword, BLAST, or Explore.</li><li>Use Tab/Shift+Tab between modules and Up/Down inside a module.</li><li>Enter edits or activates the focused primary control; Space toggles a checkbox.</li><li>In multiline sequence input, Enter inserts a newline and Ctrl+R performs the page action.</li><li>Esc closes only the top modal; visible Back/Home actions navigate the workflow.</li></ol><h2>Important keyboard distinction</h2><p><code>Ctrl+J</code> is never submit because pasted line feeds may arrive as that key. Result tables have their own cell/header control model; see Result Tables and Keyboard Controls before reviewing a large batch.</p>`,
  "Overview of the Home Page": `<h2>Choose by question</h2><table border="1" width="100%"><tr><th>Your starting material</th><th>Start here</th></tr><tr><td>Gene name, locus, identifier, annotation phrase</td><td>Keyword</td></tr><tr><td>DNA/protein sequence, FASTA, supported report URL</td><td>BLAST</td></tr><tr><td>Saved <code>.pgo</code>, loose FASTA, Newick, or viewer snapshot</td><td>Explore</td></tr></table><h2>Before pressing Start</h2><p>Check the database and version, confirm species where required, and remove empty accidental rows. Keyword requires Search term; BLAST requires FASTA/sequence content. A Symbol name is metadata, not a substitute for either required input.</p>`,
  "Building Multiple Tabs": `<h2>Use tabs intentionally</h2><p>Create a child tab when you want to preserve a result table while starting another workflow. Tabs share immutable installation files and reusable caches, but selection, current page, Canvas, and viewer service are independent. Save a <code>.pgo</code> if the work must survive closing the tab.</p><h2>Navigation boundary</h2><p>Only the root tab exposes Home. Child tabs do not treat Ctrl+O as Home. Closing a Canvas/viewer tab stops its local viewer service; it does not delete exported files.</p>`,
  "Using Keyword Search": `<h2>Complete search procedure</h2><ol><li>Open Keyword and choose the source.</li><li>Choose search type and species/release where shown.</li><li>For eligible NCBI types, choose Gene locus priority or leave None.</li><li>Enter one Search term per row; optionally set Symbol name and Gene locus.</li><li>Use normal Search for source-defined exact behavior or Wide/Broad only when explicitly offered.</li><li>Review grouped rows, inspect details, check wanted records, then Export, Canvas, or BLAST.</li></ol><h2>No-result behavior</h2><p>A precise miss stays a miss. PHgo does not silently widen the term or switch TAIR release. Retry handles acquisition failure; Back lets you choose a different explicit search mode.</p>`,
  "Keyword Search Results Table": `<h2>How to judge a row</h2><ol><li>Confirm its group title matches the submitted term.</li><li>Open View and verify source database, release/species, identifiers and description.</li><li>Distinguish <code>label_name</code> from gene/protein/transcript identifiers.</li><li>Check sequence availability before Canvas or BLAST transfer.</li><li>Select only reviewed rows.</li></ol><h2>Internal fields</h2><p><code>search_tern</code> is the preserved legacy field spelling for the submitted term. <code>geneid</code>, <code>protein_id</code>, and <code>transcript</code> represent different biological identifiers. Extra provider fields remain in details/export even when not displayed.</p>`,
  "Keyword Export": `<h2>Export procedure</h2><ol><li>Check the intended rows.</li><li>Choose Export and the destination folder.</li><li>Enter a base name and optional subfolder.</li><li>Enable XLSX for complete metadata, FASTA for sequences, PDF for analysis context, or PGO for resumable work.</li><li>Choose a FASTA header mode and generate.</li></ol><p>If no file is written, confirm that at least one output checkbox is enabled and selected rows have the required sequence. Display-column hiding never removes fields from XLSX/details.</p>`,
  "Keyword Search Connector Documentation": `<h2>How to read connector provenance</h2><p>Every connector normalizes results into shared rows but retains source-specific fields. Phytozome uses its provider interfaces; lemna uses release GFF3/AHRD and FASTA; TAIR is strictly release-bound; NCBI uses the selected Entrez database; PLAZA priority uses public versioned FTP; CYP uses a validated local PGD.</p><h2>Do not infer missing data</h2><p>Blank sequence, accession, or row URL means the source path did not supply it. <code>gene_report_url</code> is authentic only when explicitly obtained; download URLs belong in provenance fields and are not substituted as gene pages.</p>`,
  "Using BLAST": `<h2>Complete BLAST procedure</h2><ol><li>Open BLAST, choose target source and species/release.</li><li>Choose the program matching query and database molecule types.</li><li>Paste sequence/FASTA/URL rows or open a file.</li><li>Review/identify query Symbol names.</li><li>Configure external references and optional Family BLAST.</li><li>Run; follow online/local progress and recovery prompts.</li><li>Review each query table separately, filter when available, select hits, then export or add to Canvas.</li></ol><h2>Defaults passed to execution</h2><p>Core requests use the selected program and database identifiers; current standard request construction uses E-value <code>-1</code> for provider/default behavior and comparison matrix <code>BLOSUM62</code> where applicable. Provider capabilities may expose a narrower surface.</p>`,
  "All BLAST Modes": `<h2>Choose correctly</h2><p>BLASTN compares nucleotide directly. BLASTX translates a nucleotide query against protein. TBLASTN translates a nucleotide database for a protein query. BLASTP compares protein directly. Translation programs are not interchangeable with converting input in Canvas.</p><h2>Execution target</h2><p>Server means the selected provider's exposed database and polling path. Local means selected-release FASTA plus BLAST+. If both exist, lemna tries online first and falls back only after the server path is unavailable or unreliable. TAIR historical releases are local-only.</p>`,
  "Family Merger": `<h2>What grouping changes</h2><p>Grouping creates one review/export family from related query labels. Merging then optionally collapses duplicate target rows. Neither operation changes the biological hit label.</p><h2>Default normalization</h2><p>Minimum group size is 2. Leading species prefixes, trailing member numbers, number-following suffixes, terminal subtype suffixes, and inner punctuation are normalized for the derived family key. Duplicate targets are merged and the preferred hit is retained. Disable merging—not grouping—when every member hit must remain visible.</p>`,
  "Cross-Database Enhancer": `<h2>Use enrichment safely</h2><ol><li>Enable only references needed for the review.</li><li>Wait for accession prefetch, UniProt lookup, and InterPro query/hit stages.</li><li>Read blank as unavailable, not negative evidence.</li><li>Open row details to compare original, UniProt, and InterPro field groups.</li></ol><p>Enrichment does not replace original source identifiers and does not create cross-release source records. Cached negative results prevent repeated calls during the same work.</p>`,
  "BLAST Results Table": `<h2>Interpret hit statistics</h2><p><b>identity</b> is identical aligned positions; <b>align_len/query_length</b> is query coverage; <b>E-value</b> estimates chance matches (smaller is stronger); <b>bitscore</b> is normalized alignment score. Target length, canonical UniProt length, and query length are distinct denominators.</p><h2>Review order</h2><ol><li>Confirm the current query in the left run list.</li><li>Inspect strength and coverage.</li><li>Compare target identifiers/description.</li><li>Review UniProt and InterPro evidence when enabled.</li><li>Use View for full values, then set checkboxes.</li></ol>`,
  "BLAST Export": `<h2>Current versus all</h2><p>Current table exports the active query/family unit. All tables exports every retained query run, including runs hidden by navigation. Ctrl+D may preselect all scope but still opens the confirmation modal.</p><h2>FASTA content</h2><p>Family output may prepend all available query records unless <b>Prepend only first query</b> is enabled, then appends selected hit sequences. Header mode applies at the final writer boundary. Keep XLSX/PDF beside simplified FASTA to retain query-to-hit provenance.</p>`,
  "BLAST Connector Documentation": `<h2>Capability definitions</h2><p><b>server nucleotide/protein DB</b> means the provider exposes an automatable database ID. <b>local nucleotide/protein FASTA</b> means an official matching release asset can build a BLAST database. Availability controls which programs appear.</p><h2>Failure distinction</h2><p>Capability detection failure, submission failure, polling failure, local database-build failure, command failure, and successful zero hits are different outcomes. Preserve the stage text when reporting a problem.</p>`,
  "Sessions": `<h2>Save and reopen</h2><ol><li>From a result or Canvas export, enable session snapshot.</li><li>Choose a destination and write the <code>.pgo</code>.</li><li>Later choose Explore → Open session and select it.</li><li>Verify source, rows, selections and Canvas state before continuing.</li></ol><h2>Definition</h2><p>A PGO is a versioned structured snapshot with workflow context and selected durable artifacts. It is not merely renamed FASTA and not a Reactree PGV. Browser scroll, open menus, focus and panel expansion are intentionally transient.</p>`,
  "Canvas": `<h2>Hands-on Canvas workflow</h2><ol><li>Add selected Keyword/BLAST rows or choose New canvas and import FASTA.</li><li>Use the left list to switch Canvas items.</li><li>Review sequence readiness and edit <code>display_name</code> where needed.</li><li>Set green checkboxes for the shared tree/MSA set.</li><li>Export FASTA, save PGO, or open tree settings and Refresh.</li></ol><h2>Import definition</h2><p>Imported FASTA creates Canvas FASTA rows. A PHgo Canvas header restores source identity, raw row/title and locked display name; generic headers retain only what can be parsed honestly.</p>`,
  "PHgo FASTA Header Formats": `<h2>Round-trip checklist</h2><ol><li>Use PHgo Canvas when the file will return to Canvas.</li><li>Use PHgo Lite for compact human-readable exchange.</li><li>Use Original only when downstream identity depends on the provider header.</li><li>Use Minimal or Display-name-only only with companion provenance.</li><li>Never manually remove <code>~</code> placeholders from a full PHgo header; field position is semantic.</li></ol><h2>Parser definition</h2><p>The full parser requires exactly three slash-separated self fields and recognizes query marker, row, BLAST-source, and Canvas group shapes. Malformed group counts or missing required separators fall back to non-PHgo handling rather than partial guessing.</p>`,
  "Phylogenetic Trees": `<h2>Recommended first tree</h2><ol><li>Select at least two sequence-ready Canvas rows.</li><li>Choose Protein unless you have real nucleotide/CDS for every selected row.</li><li>Start with ClustalW or MUSCLE defaults and Neighbor-Joining.</li><li>Refresh and inspect runtime errors before changing methods.</li><li>Open Reactree, review labels/topology, then save PGO and export the desired viewer artifact.</li></ol><h2>Parameter rule</h2><p>Every editable registered alignment/tree parameter is serialized into the runtime request. Read-only rows describe the selected MEGA path. A parameter irrelevant to the chosen method is not a hidden global switch.</p>`,
  "Multiple Sequence Alignment": `<h2>Recommended editing cycle</h2><ol><li>Open MSA after a successful tree refresh.</li><li>Inspect names, gaps, groups, annotations and features.</li><li>Use Save to persist browser/Jalview state without touching Canvas.</li><li>Use Apply only when edits should return to Canvas and affect shared state.</li><li>Use File → Export image... for a publication graphic.</li></ol><h2>Residue ratio definition</h2><p>The displayed numerator counts characters that are not gap, blank, or terminal-marker characters. The denominator is the current aligned row length. It is visual metadata only and never becomes a FASTA name.</p>`,
  "Standalone Tree Viewer": `<h2>Open procedure</h2><ol><li>Choose Explore → Tree viewer browser.</li><li>Use Open... or enter exactly one supported path/URL.</li><li>Press Open and work in the launched browser page.</li><li>Save PGV for viewer state or export graphics/Newick as offered.</li><li>Return and leave the input page only after saving; leaving stops that viewer service.</li></ol>`,
  "Main Interface and Data Sources": `<h2>Decision checklist</h2><p>Choose the source that owns the release you intend to cite. Set species before entering a large batch so every row has the correct context. For NCBI, first choose the Entrez database whose records contain the identifiers/fields you need. For CYP, decide whether species is per-row or applied to remaining rows.</p>`,
  "Input Formats and Batch Rules": `<h2>Before submitting a batch</h2><ol><li>Remove accidental blank records.</li><li>Ensure FASTA records begin with <code>&gt;</code> and sequence lines belong to the correct header.</li><li>Use one supported URL per item.</li><li>Ensure label count matches item count.</li><li>Check molecule type against BLAST program.</li></ol>`,
  "Result Tables and Keyboard Controls": `<h2>Safe large-table routine</h2><p>First sort only the evidence column you intend to inspect, then use View rather than trusting truncated display text, set checkboxes, and export. Sorting changes presentation order but not source row identity; row numbers allow you to report the reviewed record.</p>`,
  "External References, Family BLAST, and Filtering": `<h2>Recommended use</h2><p>Run once without aggressive optional identity/E-value rules, inspect why rows were rejected, then tighten thresholds for the scientific question. Because Filter changes selection suggestions rather than deleting evidence, retain XLSX or PGO if auditability matters.</p>`,
  "Exports, Reports, Cache, and Recovery": `<h2>What to keep</h2><p>For a reproducible analysis keep the PGO, XLSX, selected FASTA, PDF report, and the software/source release identifiers together. Cache is disposable acceleration data; it is never the sole archival copy.</p>`
};

// The pages below are literal copies of dc_sample.html with only these two
// portions replaced. Keep the old tags, indentation, webbot navigation, and
// table layout intact; this site intentionally does not use a modern template.
